  "id": "Compound::DB00963 - Gene::5743",
  "verbalization": "Bromfenac, a cyclooxygenase inhibitor, is linked to Ptgs2 through multiple pathways, primarily through its inclusion in the class of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). Both Bromfenac and its related compounds, such as Nepafenac and Oxaprozin, bind to Ptgs2, a key enzyme in the inflammatory response. This binding is supported by the lowest common ancestor relationship between Bromfenac and Nepafenac, which both fall under the broader category of Organonitrogen Compounds and Nonsteroidal Antiinflammatory Drugs. The shared mechanism of action—binding to Ptgs2—suggests a common pharmacological pathway, reinforcing the relationship between Bromfenac and Ptgs2. The evidence also highlights the role of cyclooxygenase inhibition in modulating inflammation, which is a central function of Ptgs2. While the exact mechanism of how Bromfenac directly influences Ptgs2 remains to be fully elucidated, the consistent binding and functional relationship across different NSAIDs provide a robust foundation for the proposed connection.",
